---
name: extract-requirements
description: Extract functional and non-functional requirements from text documents into a generated Requirements Specification (FRS) Excel workbook, following the BABOK requirements classification schema. Use when the user wants to pull requirements out of notes, specifications or a business case; author or complete non-functional requirements (performance, availability, security, usability, compliance and the other BABOK quality attributes); convert informal requirement descriptions into structured form; categorise and prioritise requirements (MoSCoW, user stories); or populate the standard PRJ-Functional Requirements Specification format.
---

# Extract Requirements

Turns a text document into a two-sheet Requirements Specification workbook using
`functional_requirement.py`. The reference framework is
[BABOK_requirements.html](../../rules/BABOK_requirements.html) — read the framing
below before writing any requirement, and open the reference itself when a
classification call is genuinely unclear.

## BABOK framing

This workbook holds **solution requirements** only — the third level of the BABOK
classification schema (reference, Part 1). Solution requirements split into
exactly two kinds, and the workbook gives each its own worksheet:

| | Functional Requirements sheet | Non-Functional Requirements sheet |
|---|---|---|
| Defines | Capabilities the solution must have, in terms of behaviour and the information it will manage — the WHAT | Conditions under which the solution must remain effective, or qualities it must have — HOW WELL |
| Reads like | "The system must record the size selected for each PPE item." | "95% of picking slips shall generate within 3 seconds at 60 concurrent inductions." |
| Verified by | Functional / user acceptance testing | Performance, security and quality-attribute testing against a stated measure |

**Business requirements** (the WHY) belong in the Sponsor Brief, **stakeholder
requirements** in the elicitation notes, and **transition requirements**
(migration, training, parallel running) in the implementation plan. Do not put
them in this workbook. If the source document mixes them in, leave them out and
say so when you report — a business goal on the functional sheet is something a
vendor cannot respond to.

Both sheets get a definition table on the Instructions sheet, so the reader can
see where each sheet sits in the schema.

## Process

1. **Identify the input file.** Ask the user which text file contains the
   requirements to extract.

2. **Check whether the input is already in requirement format.**
   - Contains explicit "must", "should", "shall", "will" statements → go to step 4.
   - Business case, high-level description or informal document → go to step 3.

3. **Create formatted requirements** (required for business cases).
   - Read the source document to understand the proposed solution and its capabilities.
   - Create `<original_filename>_Requirements.txt` in the same directory.
   - Write structured requirements:
     - Use "The system must…", "The platform must…", "The system should…" language
     - Organise by phases, features or functional areas
     - Number each requirement (1, 2, 3, …)
     - Group related requirements under descriptive headings
   - Example transformation:
     - Business case: "The platform will automate document analysis"
     - Requirement: "The system must automatically read and interpret supplier documentation."
   - Then work through the [Non-functional requirements worksheet](#non-functional-requirements-worksheet)
     as a **separate pass**, not as an afterthought while writing functional
     requirements — the whole point of the category checklist is that it makes you
     consider quality attributes nobody volunteered.

   Use a **two-level heading structure** so CATEGORY and SUB-CATEGORY are both
   meaningful (see [Category and sub-category rules](#category-and-sub-category-rules)):
   - `MODULE: <name>` on its own line for the top level → becomes CATEGORY
   - `A. <NAME>`, `B. <NAME>` (or markdown `## <name>`) for the second level → becomes SUB-CATEGORY
   - Pick module names from the real solution structure (delivery modules, phases,
     subsystems), and section names for the distinct capability within each module

   ```
   MODULE: WORKFLOW

   A. SIZING AND ORDER CAPTURE

   1. The system must record the worker preferred name...
   ```

4. **Run the extraction.**

   ```bash
   python .claude/skills/scripts/functional_requirement.py <formatted_requirements_file> [project_name]
   ```

   - `<formatted_requirements_file>` — path to the formatted requirements text file (e.g. `*_Requirements.txt`)
   - `[project_name]` — optional; defaults to the input filename

5. **Review what the script did.** It will:
   - Extract requirements by pattern matching
   - Route each one to the Functional or Non-Functional sheet from the document's own headings (see [step 4 of the worksheet](#step-4--how-routing-works-and-why-it-is-structural))
   - Categorise functional requirements (Authentication, Data Management, UI, Integration, …) and non-functional requirements by BABOK s10.30 category
   - Assign a MoSCoW priority (Must / Should / Could / Won't) from keywords
   - Build the workbook in code via `frs_template.py` — no template file is read, so there is nothing to locate or copy
   - Write a timestamped Excel file to `requirements/` as `FRS_{project_name}_{timestamp}.xlsx`
   - Print `WARNING:` lines for quality defects and `REVIEW:` lines for requirements that may be on the wrong sheet

6. **Verify the output** and report:
   - Number of functional and non-functional requirements found, and the output file location
   - Any warnings if no requirements were detected
   - **Act on every `WARNING:` line** by fixing the source text file and re-running. A missing success measure or an off-vocabulary NFR category is a requirement the vendor cannot be held to.
   - **Assess every `REVIEW:` line** and either move the requirement under `MODULE: NON-FUNCTIONAL` with a measure, or say why it is functional. Report the ones you left in place with the reason — do not silently ignore them.
   - Confirm no SUB-CATEGORY merely restates its CATEGORY. If redundant pairs appear, fix the headings in the requirements text file and re-run rather than editing the Excel file by hand.
   - Confirm every functional requirement row has a SUB-CATEGORY. A blank column B means a section heading duplicated its module name and was suppressed — split that section into named capabilities and re-run.
   - Confirm borders, fills and the MRL Priority / COMPLIANCE dropdowns run to the last requirement row on **both** sheets.
   - Confirm every requirement row is fully ruled, including a bottom border on CATEGORY and SUB-CATEGORY, so those columns match the ID column.
   - Confirm MRL Priority values are Must / Should / Could / Won't. Any other vocabulary (High/Medium/Low) fails the data validation.
   - Confirm the Non-Functional Requirements sheet is not empty. An empty sheet almost always means the worksheet pass was skipped, not that the solution has no quality requirements.
   - If the script reports an error, no output file is written — the workbook is only saved once every row is populated. Fix the reported cause and re-run; do not go looking for a partial file to repair.

## Non-functional requirements worksheet

Work through this as its own pass. BABOK's reason for prescribing a category
checklist (reference, Part 4 and Part 13) is that stakeholders describe the
functionality they want and go quiet about quality — so a non-functional
requirement is far more often *missing* than wrong. Late discovery is expensive:
a response-time target found after build means rearchitecting, not
reconfiguring.

### Step 1 — Walk the categories

Take each BABOK s10.30 category in turn and answer its question against **this**
solution. The categories are defined on the workbook's Instructions sheet, in
code in `NFR_CATEGORIES` in `frs_template.py`, and in the reference at
[Part 4: BABOK's Categorization of Non-Functional Requirements](../../rules/BABOK_requirements.html).

| Category | Question to put to the stakeholder |
|---|---|
| Availability | When must it be usable, and what downtime is tolerable in that window? |
| Compatibility | What must it work with — systems, devices, browsers, data standards? |
| Compliance | What legislation, regulation, audit or standing policy binds it? |
| Business Continuity | What happens to the business when it is down, and how fast must it come back? |
| Functionality | How accurate or complete must its outputs be? |
| Maintainability | What must the business be able to change without a developer? |
| Operability | How is it monitored and supported once live, and by whom? |
| Performance Efficiency | How fast, at what volume, measured where? |
| Portability | What environments must it run in? |
| Reliability | What failure or error rate is tolerable? |
| Scalability | What growth must it absorb without breaching the other targets? |
| Security | Who must be kept out of what, and how is access proven? |
| Usability | Who uses it, with what training, to what accessibility standard? |

**Record "not applicable" decisions in your report to the user**, with the
reason. A category nobody considered and a category deliberately excluded look
identical in an empty workbook, and only one of them is safe.

### Step 2 — Write each one with a success measure

Every non-functional requirement needs a **measure** (reference, Part 3 "The
Challenge of Measurement", and Part 10 "Common Measurement Approaches").
Stakeholders volunteer quality in vague terms ("must be fast", "must be easy to
use"); a requirement that stays vague is untestable, and the vendor is being
asked to agree to something nobody can hold them to. Translate:

| Do not write | Write |
|---|---|
| The system must be fast. | The system must generate picking slips without delaying Day 1 PPE issue. **Measure:** 95% of slips generated within 3 seconds at 60 concurrent inductions. |
| The system must be available. | The system must be available across the induction operating window. **Measure:** 99.5% availability 05:00–18:00 AWST weekdays, excluding notified maintenance. |
| The system must be user-friendly. | A new PPE Assistant must be able to complete a worker sizing unaided. **Measure:** First unaided sizing completed within 4 minutes after a single training session. |
| The system must be secure. | Worker personal information must be readable only by holders of the PPE Assistant or Warehouse Officer role. **Measure:** Access attempt by any other role denied and logged, evidenced by penetration test. |

A measure needs a number or a named standard (WCAG 2.1 AA, ISO 27001), the
condition it holds under (load, volume, time window), and where it is observed.
"Fast enough" is not a measure. If the business genuinely cannot yet supply a
figure at Concept stage, write the measure as an explicit open question —
`Measure: TBC in Define - target to be set against current-state baseline` —
rather than leaving it blank.

### Step 3 — Format for extraction

Non-functional requirements go under a module the script recognises, with one
section per BABOK category, and a `Measure:` line indented beneath each
requirement:

```
MODULE: NON-FUNCTIONAL

P. PERFORMANCE EFFICIENCY

76. The system must generate name-printing and picking slips without delaying Day 1 PPE issue.
    Measure: 95% of slips generated within 3 seconds at 60 concurrent inductions.
    Sub-category: Slip generation

77. The system must remain usable at peak induction volumes at 18 Hasler Drive.
    Measure: No degradation in response time at 120 workers sized per day.

Q. AVAILABILITY

78. The system must be available across the induction operating window.
    Measure: 99.5% availability 05:00-18:00 AWST weekdays, excluding notified windows.
```

Attribute lines recognised beneath a requirement, all optional except `Measure:`:

- `Measure:` (or `Success measure:` / `Target:`) → SUCCESS MEASURE column
- `Category:` → overrides the BABOK category derived from the section heading
- `Sub-category:` → SUB-CATEGORY column

Attribute lines are not extracted as requirements of their own. The block ends at
the first line that is not an attribute, so keep them directly beneath their
requirement with no blank line in between.

### Step 4 — How routing works, and why it is structural

A requirement lands on the Non-Functional sheet **only** if it sits under
`MODULE: NON-FUNCTIONAL` or under a section heading tagged `[NFR]`, e.g.
`N. RESPONSE TIMES [NFR]`. Nothing is routed on keywords.

This is deliberate. Under BABOK, "the system must authenticate users against the
MinRes identity service" is *functional* — it is behaviour the solution performs
— even though it is about security. So is "the system must integrate with the
finance system". A keyword router would drag both onto the quality-attribute
sheet and lose them. The distinction is what the requirement *is*, not which
vocabulary it borrows, and only the author knows that.

The script still scans functional requirements for quality-threshold phrasing and
prints `REVIEW:` advisories. Those are prompts for your judgement, not
instructions — resolve each by editing headings in the source document, never by
editing the Excel file.

### Step 5 — Verify against BABOK's nine quality characteristics

Apply these to every requirement on both sheets before you report done
(reference, Part 6). They are reproduced on the Instructions sheet for the
reader.

- **Atomic** — one requirement, one thought. Split "the system must be fast and secure".
- **Complete** — enough for the audience to act on, with no gap left to interpretation.
- **Consistent** — no contradictions; same term for the same thing throughout.
- **Concise** — plain and brief.
- **Feasible** — achievable within known budget, timeline and technology. A 99.99% target on a solution with no redundancy budget is not a requirement, it is a wish.
- **Unambiguous** — exactly one reading by every stakeholder.
- **Testable** — for a non-functional requirement this *is* the success measure.
- **Prioritised** — MoSCoW, in the MRL Priority column.
- **Understandable** — the business's own language.

Also check for **conflicting** non-functional requirements. Performance and
security trade off against each other, as do availability and cost. Where two
targets pull against each other, surface the trade-off to the user rather than
writing both down and leaving the vendor to discover it.

## Category and sub-category rules

CATEGORY and SUB-CATEGORY must each carry information the other does not. The
script enforces this, but the headings you write determine the quality.

**How the functional columns are populated** (in preference order):

1. `MODULE: x` heading → CATEGORY; nearest section heading → SUB-CATEGORY
2. Section heading → CATEGORY; keyword-derived category → SUB-CATEGORY
3. Keyword-derived category → CATEGORY, SUB-CATEGORY blank

**How the non-functional columns are populated:**

1. CATEGORY is always a BABOK s10.30 quality attribute, from a `Category:` line
   or from the section heading mapped onto the canonical vocabulary. Section
   headings are matched loosely, so `N. RESPONSE TIMES` resolves to Performance
   Efficiency — but an unrecognised heading is passed through verbatim with a
   `WARNING:`, so rename it or add a `Category:` line.
2. SUB-CATEGORY names the part of the solution the threshold applies to. This is
   what distinguishes two Performance requirements from each other, so supply it
   with a `Sub-category:` line wherever a category holds more than one requirement.

**A blank SUB-CATEGORY is correct and preferred** over filler. The script blanks
any sub-category that:

- exactly repeats the category ("Performance" / "Performance")
- restates it with padding ("General" / "General Requirements", "User Interface" / "Interface", "Security" / "Security Controls")
- reduces to nothing but filler words after stripping "general", "requirements", "system", "management", "controls", "interface", "analytics"

Never reintroduce self-restating pairs. If a whole section would land in
"General", the section heading is too vague — name the actual capability.

**Do not let a section heading repeat its module name.** `MODULE: WORKER
NOTIFICATION` followed by `F. WORKER NOTIFICATION` yields a blank SUB-CATEGORY
for every requirement in it. Split single-section modules into the distinct
capabilities they contain — e.g. Notification Triggers, Channel and Delivery,
Notification Records. Every module needs at least two differently-named
sections, or its sub-category column stays empty.

## Table formatting

`frs_template.py` builds the workbook and styles each requirement row as it is
written, so formatting always covers exactly the rows that exist — there are no
pre-formatted example rows to run out of and no placeholder rows to clear.

Handled automatically — do not work around these by hand:

- `style_requirement_row()` applies borders, fills, fonts and alignment to every populated row on either sheet, choosing alignment by column *name* so the two sheets' differing column orders both come out right
- Data validation dropdowns (MRL Priority, COMPLIANCE) are attached to the first data row by the builder and stretched to the last requirement row by the extractor
- COMPLIANCE and COMMENTS carry the tan vendor-input fill and are left blank
- The Non-Functional sheet carries a tan `Vendor Name:` cell at B1, as the general instructions require, so its header sits on row 4 and data starts at row 5
- Header rows are frozen on both sheets
- Priorities are written as MoSCoW (Must / Should / Could / Won't) to match the MRL Priority dropdown vocabulary

**Every cell in the table is ruled on all four sides**, CATEGORY and
SUB-CATEGORY included, so those columns look consistent with the ID column
rather than merging consecutive same-category rows into an unruled block. Keep
it that way — do not reintroduce grouped borders.

## Requirement detection patterns

The script looks for:

- Explicit requirement keywords: "must allow", "shall provide", "should enable"
- System/user statements: "The system must…", "User should…"
- User stories: "As a user I want to…"
- Numbered/bulleted lists with requirement keywords

Attribute lines (`Measure:`, `Category:`, `Sub-category:`) are excluded from
detection and attached to the requirement above them instead.

## Categories detected

Functional requirements (keyword-derived, used only where headings are absent):
Authentication (login, passwords, user accounts), Data Management (database,
storage, CRUD), User Interface (UI, displays, forms, buttons), Integration (APIs,
third-party systems), Reporting (dashboards, analytics, exports), Security
(encryption, authorization, access control), Performance (speed, scalability,
response time), Notification (alerts, emails, messages), General
(uncategorised).

Non-functional requirements use the BABOK s10.30 vocabulary instead:
Availability, Compatibility, Compliance, Business Continuity, Functionality,
Maintainability, Operability, Performance Efficiency, Portability, Reliability,
Scalability, Security, Usability. The sheet is sorted into that order so it reads
as a coverage checklist — a category with no rows is a visible gap.

## Scripts

Both files live in [.claude/skills/scripts/](../scripts/) and must stay together —
the extractor imports the builder from its own directory:

- [functional_requirement.py](../scripts/functional_requirement.py) — extracts requirements, routes them to a sheet and writes the rows
- [frs_template.py](../scripts/frs_template.py) — builds the workbook (all three sheets, styling, dropdowns, the BABOK reference tables, Compliance and Priority definitions) in code

Each requirements sheet is described by one dict in `frs_template.py`
(`FUNCTIONAL_SHEET`, `NON_FUNCTIONAL_SHEET`) covering its columns, widths, header
row and ID prefix. To add a column, add it there — styling and dropdowns follow
from the column names, so nothing else needs touching.

There is no `.xlsx` template file. Everything the workbook needs is defined in
`frs_template.py`, so the skill is self-contained and openpyxl is the only
external requirement. Run `python .claude/skills/scripts/frs_template.py <path>`
to write a blank workbook if you need to inspect the layout.

## Troubleshooting

- **No requirements found** — check the input file contains requirement-like language.
- **Non-Functional sheet empty** — nothing in the source sits under `MODULE: NON-FUNCTIONAL` or a `[NFR]`-tagged section. Routing is structural; quality-attribute wording alone will not move a requirement across.
- **SUCCESS MEASURE blank** — the requirement has no `Measure:` line directly beneath it, or a blank line separated them.
- **NFR CATEGORY looks wrong** — the section heading matched a different alias first. Add an explicit `Category:` line to settle it.
- **`ModuleNotFoundError: frs_template`** — `frs_template.py` is missing from the same directory as `functional_requirement.py`; restore it there.
- **Encoding errors** — the script attempts UTF-8 first, then falls back to latin-1.

## Example usage

```bash
# Extract from a text file with auto-detected project name
python .claude/skills/scripts/functional_requirement.py requirements.txt

# Extract with a specific project name
python .claude/skills/scripts/functional_requirement.py specs.txt "CRM System"
```

## Dependencies

- Python 3.x with the `openpyxl` library installed
- Write permissions for the `requirements/` output directory
- [.claude/rules/BABOK_requirements.html](../../rules/BABOK_requirements.html) — the requirements classification schema, NFR categories and quality characteristics this skill implements
